<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#amharic">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amharic</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Amharic language</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amharic is a Semitic language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geez">Ge'ez script</a>, and the working language of the Ethiopian federal government. It was the language of the court and administration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Its imposition in schools and government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> during the federation of the 1950s was one of the grievances that fed Eritrean nationalism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amharic.jpg" alt="Amharic" width="320" height="260" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pikkupapupata · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ethiopia_-_Amharic-speaking_regions.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
